        <div role="region" aria-labelledby="e-n-accordion-item-3900" class="elementor-element elementor-element-99b3d7f e-con-full e-flex e-con e-child">
        <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-e60e9eb elementor-widget elementor-widget-text-editor">
                  According to Audible’s data, <span style="color: #00d4ff;"><strong>1.25x and 1.5x are the most popular speeds</strong></span> among regular listeners. Most beginners start at 1x and gradually increase. Power listeners often settle at 1.75x–2x for fiction and stay at 1.25x–1.5x for dense non-fiction.                </div>
        </div>
          </details>
            <details id="e-n-accordion-item-3901" class="e-n-accordion-item">
        <summary class="e-n-accordion-item-title" data-accordion-index="2" tabindex="-1" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="e-n-accordion-item-3901">
          <span class="e-n-accordion-item-title-header"><h3 class="e-n-accordion-item-title-text"> Does listening speed affect comprehension? </h3></span>
              <span class="e-n-accordion-item-title-icon">
      <span class="e-opened"><i aria-hidden="true" class="icon icon-cross-circle"></i></span>
      <span class="e-closed"><i aria-hidden="true" class="icon icon-plus-circle"></i></span>
    </span>
            </summary>
        <div role="region" aria-labelledby="e-n-accordion-item-3901" class="elementor-element elementor-element-4177d0a e-con-full e-flex e-con e-child">
        <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-7d471aa elementor-widget elementor-widget-text-editor">
                  Research shows comprehension <span style="color: #00d4ff;"><strong>stays high up to about 1.5x–2x</strong></span> for most listeners, especially with practice. Above 2x, comprehension drops for most people — particularly for complex or dense material. Light fiction and familiar topics can be consumed at higher speeds without significant loss.                </div>
        </div>
          </details>
            <details id="e-n-accordion-item-3902" class="e-n-accordion-item">
        <summary class="e-n-accordion-item-title" data-accordion-index="3" tabindex="-1" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="e-n-accordion-item-3902">
          <span class="e-n-accordion-item-title-header"><h3 class="e-n-accordion-item-title-text"> How is audiobook listening time calculated? </h3></span>
              <span class="e-n-accordion-item-title-icon">
      <span class="e-opened"><i aria-hidden="true" class="icon icon-cross-circle"></i></span>
      <span class="e-closed"><i aria-hidden="true" class="icon icon-plus-circle"></i></span>
    </span>
            </summary>
        <div role="region" aria-labelledby="e-n-accordion-item-3902" class="elementor-element elementor-element-e87db77 e-con-full e-flex e-con e-child">
        <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-57106de elementor-widget elementor-widget-text-editor">
                  Simple division: <span style="color: #00d4ff;"><strong>Listening Time = Original Length ÷ Speed.</strong></span> A 10-hour book at 1.5x = 10 ÷ 1.5 = 6.67 hours = 6 hours 40 minutes. Our calculator does this for any length and speed combination instantly.                </div>
        </div>
          </details>
            <details id="e-n-accordion-item-3903" class="e-n-accordion-item">
        <summary class="e-n-accordion-item-title" data-accordion-index="4" tabindex="-1" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="e-n-accordion-item-3903">
          <span class="e-n-accordion-item-title-header"><h3 class="e-n-accordion-item-title-text"> Which apps support the widest speed range? </h3></span>
              <span class="e-n-accordion-item-title-icon">
      <span class="e-opened"><i aria-hidden="true" class="icon icon-cross-circle"></i></span>
      <span class="e-closed"><i aria-hidden="true" class="icon icon-plus-circle"></i></span>
    </span>
            </summary>
        <div role="region" aria-labelledby="e-n-accordion-item-3903" class="elementor-element elementor-element-284fbcb e-con-full e-flex e-con e-child">
        <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-df1d8a6 elementor-widget elementor-widget-text-editor">
                  <span style="color: #00d4ff;"><strong>Audible</strong> </span>supports 0.5x to 3.5x · <span style="color: #00d4ff;"><strong>Libby</strong> </span>supports 1x to 3x · <span style="color: #00d4ff;"><strong>Apple Books</strong></span> supports 0.75x to 2x · <span style="color: #00d4ff;"><strong>Spotify</strong> </span>supports 0.5x to 3.5x · <span style="color: #00d4ff;"><strong>Google Play Books</strong></span> supports 0.5x to 3x. Most apps use pitch-correction so voices don’t distort at higher speeds.                </div>
        </div>
          </details>
            <details id="e-n-accordion-item-3904" class="e-n-accordion-item">
        <summary class="e-n-accordion-item-title" data-accordion-index="5" tabindex="-1" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="e-n-accordion-item-3904">
          <span class="e-n-accordion-item-title-header"><h3 class="e-n-accordion-item-title-text"> How do I trainyself to listen faster? </h3></span>
              <span class="e-n-accordion-item-title-icon">
      <span class="e-opened"><i aria-hidden="true" class="icon icon-cross-circle"></i></span>
      <span class="e-closed"><i aria-hidden="true" class="icon icon-plus-circle"></i></span>
    </span>
            </summary>
        <div role="region" aria-labelledby="e-n-accordion-item-3904" class="elementor-element elementor-element-1b1a4ee e-con-full e-flex e-con e-child">
        <div class="elementor-element elementor-element-5169f32 elementor-widget elementor-widget-text-editor">
                  Start at <span style="color: #00d4ff;"><strong>1.1x or 1.25x</strong> </span>for 1–2 weeks until it feels completely natural. Then bump to 1.5x. Most people find each jump takes 3–7 days to adjust. Re-listen to a familiar book at a higher speed — your brain already knows the story so it can process faster speech more easily.                </div>
        </div>
          </details>
